{"timestamp": "2026-08-16T08:00:26.215661", "tool": "write_file", "args": {"content": "Johannes Gutenberg invented the movable-type printing press around 1440, revolutionizing book production by replacing slow, manual copying with a fast, reusable mechanical process. This technology, which utilized oil-based ink and reusable metal type, spread rapidly across Europe. Historians credit the printing press with significantly boosting literacy, standardizing languages, and enabling the swift dissemination of ideas during major periods like the Renaissance and Reformation.", "filename": "demo_article_summary.txt"}, "allowed": true, "reason": "ok"}
